From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 07:02:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] libril: stop exporting nanopb so the QTI blob can bind its
 own

QTI's libril-qc-qmi-1.so does not carry a nanopb; it imports pb_encode/pb_decode and hands over
message descriptors that were generated against nanopb 0.2.8, the version Android 7.1.1 shipped.

Between 0.2.8 and 0.3.x nanopb inserted PB_LTYPE_BOOL at 0x00 and shifted every other PB_LTYPE_* up
by one:

    0.2.8   VARINT 0x00  UVARINT 0x01  SVARINT 0x02  ...  STRING 0x06  SUBMESSAGE 0x07
    0.3.9   BOOL   0x00  VARINT  0x01  UVARINT 0x02  ...  STRING 0x07  SUBMESSAGE 0x08

So the modern runtime reads every field of those descriptors as the next type along. On hardware that
is 10 messages received, 10 decode failures, 0 successes, and nothing sent back -- with
"Decoding failed: missing required field" on the way in and "Encoding failed: invalid data_size" on
the way out. No PB_FIELD_8/16/32BIT setting can fix it; all three were built and tested, and 32-bit
additionally SIGSEGVs because a misread LTYPE sends the decoder chasing a submessage pointer.

libril cannot simply be built against 0.2.8: librilutils generates libril's own SAP descriptors at
build time with the modern generator, so libril needs the modern runtime for itself. The two coexist
by keeping the nanopb version AOSP uses and hiding it from the dynamic symbol table, which leaves the
blob free to bind to a 0.2.8 copy shipped alongside. Static linkage means libril's SAP path is
unaffected by the visibility change.
---
 libril/Android.mk     | 10 ++++++++++
 libril/libril.map.txt | 13 +++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 libril/libril.map.txt

diff --git a/libril/Android.mk b/libril/Android.mk
index c39c5a9..bebf0ba 100644
--- a/libril/Android.mk
+++ b/libril/Android.mk
@@ -24,6 +24,15 @@ LOCAL_SHARED_LIBRARIES := \
     android.hardware.radio@1.1 \
     libhidlbase \
 
+# nanopb stays as AOSP has it -- librilutils generates libril's own SAP descriptors with the modern
+# 32-bit generator, so this runtime has to match those.
+#
+# What changes is that libril no longer EXPORTS nanopb (see libril.map.txt). QTI's
+# libril-qc-qmi-1.so imports pb_encode/pb_decode rather than carrying its own, and its descriptors
+# were generated against nanopb 0.2.8 -- the version Android 7.1.1 shipped. Between 0.2.8 and 0.3.x
+# nanopb inserted PB_LTYPE_BOOL at 0x00 and shifted every other PB_LTYPE_* up by one, so the modern
+# runtime reads every field of those descriptors as the next type along and nothing decodes. Hiding
+# the symbols lets the blob bind to libnanopb_legacy instead.
 LOCAL_STATIC_LIBRARIES := \
     libprotobuf-c-nano-enable_malloc-32bit \
 
@@ -44,6 +53,7 @@ LOCAL_C_INCLUDES += $(LOCAL_PATH)/../include
 LOCAL_EXPORT_C_INCLUDE_DIRS := $(LOCAL_PATH)/../include
 
 LOCAL_MODULE:= libril
+LOCAL_LDFLAGS += -Wl,--version-script=$(LOCAL_PATH)/libril.map.txt
 LOCAL_LICENSE_KINDS:= SPDX-license-identifier-Apache-2.0
 LOCAL_LICENSE_CONDITIONS:= notice
 LOCAL_NOTICE_FILE:= $(LOCAL_PATH)/NOTICE
diff --git a/libril/libril.map.txt b/libril/libril.map.txt
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..84e74dc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libril/libril.map.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# libril exports everything it always did, except nanopb.
+#
+# QTI's libril-qc-qmi-1.so imports pb_encode/pb_decode and, in a single-namespace process, would
+# bind them to whichever library exports them first -- libril, whose nanopb is the modern 0.3.x.
+# Its descriptors were generated against 0.2.8, where every PB_LTYPE_* is one lower, so nothing it
+# sends decodes. Hiding these here lets the blob bind to libnanopb_legacy instead, while libril goes
+# on using its own copy internally for SAP: static linkage resolves regardless of export visibility.
+{
+  global:
+    *;
+  local:
+    pb_*;
+};
